export const GLOSSARY = {
  "offload": "Moving pressure away from a painful or at-risk spot by reshaping the insole under it.",
  "metatarsal heads": "The knuckle-like bone ends at the ball of the foot, where the toes begin. Often shortened to \u201cmet heads.\u201d",
  "durometer": "A measure of material firmness. Lower number = softer, higher = firmer.",
  "plantar fascia": "The strong band of tissue running from the heel to the toes along the bottom of the foot.",
  "neuropathy": "Loss of feeling, usually from diabetes. A patient may not feel a sore forming, so pressure must be managed for them.",
  "Charcot foot": "A diabetic complication where bones weaken and the arch can collapse, creating a rocker-shaped foot that needs total-contact support.",
  "sesamoids": "Two small bones under the big-toe joint that act like a pulley. They take enormous pressure when walking.",
  "cuboid": "A cube-shaped bone on the outer midfoot. When irritated it causes lateral midfoot pain.",
  "hammertoes": "Toes stuck in a bent, claw-like curl at the middle joint. The raised knuckle rubs the shoe and the toe tip drives into the sole.",
  "hallux": "The big toe.",
  "Morton's neuroma": "A pinched, thickened nerve between the met heads \u2014 usually between the 3rd and 4th \u2014 that burns or tingles.",
  "ulcer": "An open pressure sore. For diabetic feet, preventing ulcers is the whole mission.",
  "total contact": "An insole shaped to touch the entire sole evenly, so no single spot carries extra load.",
  "met bar": "A raised ridge that runs across the insole just behind all the met heads.",
  "met pad": "A dome-shaped raise just behind the met heads that spreads and lifts them.",
  "valgus / varus": "Tilt of the foot: valgus tips outward-in, varus tips inward-out. Wedges correct the tilt.",
  "intrinsic / extrinsic": "Two ways to build a wedge. Extrinsic: a separate printed TPU slab under a flat, untilted shell \u2014 the angle lives on the slab. Intrinsic: the tilt is baked into the shell itself, so there is no separate part.",
  "sulcus": "The groove just behind the toes, where they join the forefoot. Designers use the sulcus line as the front cutting boundary for top covers and shell extensions.",
  "calcaneus": "The heel bone. The heel cup is shaped to cradle the natural fat pad underneath it and keep it from squeezing sideways.",
  "base of the fifth": "The bony bump at the base of the fifth metatarsal, on the outer midfoot \u2014 a common pressure point in narrow shoes. Standard relief here is 3\u20133.5 mm deep.",
  "P-Cell": "A soft foam used as the skin-contact layer on diabetic inserts. Its job is cushioning and friction reduction \u2014 protecting fragile, high-risk skin.",
  "Poron": "A denser foam used for shock absorption and durability. Often the very top layer on three-layer builds, over a P-Cell middle.",
  "UCBL": "A deep, rigid orthotic shell (named for the UC Biomechanics Laboratory) that grips the heel and midfoot to control motion. Finished with a soft Puff foam top cover.",
  "navicular": "The prominent bone on the inner midfoot. Offloaded with an oval relief for accessory navicular, stress fracture, or posterior-tibial tendon pain.",
  "neuroma": "A pinched, thickened nerve between the met heads \u2014 usually the 3rd\u20134th interspace (Morton's neuroma). Feels like walking on a pebble or a burning fold in the sock. Treated with a neuroma pad that spreads the met heads apart.",
  "fibroma": "A benign fibrous nodule on the plantar fascia, usually mid-arch. Offloaded with a well placed precisely over the nodule \u2014 position accuracy is everything.",
  "medial / lateral": "The two sides of the foot. Medial = the inner side (big toe, arch); lateral = the outer side (little toe). This does not flip from left foot to right foot. Every placement instruction in a prescription uses these two words.",
  "Medial": "Inner side = big-toe side. Same on both feet.",
  "Lateral": "Outer side = little-toe side. Same on both feet.",
  "1st Met-Head": "The metatarsal head on the big-toe (medial) end of the ball-of-the-foot row.",
  "5th Met-Head": "The metatarsal head on the little-toe (lateral) end of the ball-of-the-foot row.",
  "Met-Head Pad": "The fatty cushioning tissue underneath the metatarsal heads \u2014 the natural cushion under the ball of the foot.",
  "Medial Arch": "The inner longitudinal arch, from the forefoot toward the heel. The arch you see from the inside of the foot.",
  "Peroneal Arch": "The smaller outer-foot arch, between the heel and the Base of 5th, along the lateral border.",
  "Heel Pad": "The fatty cushioning tissue beneath the calcaneus (heel bone). Heel = foundation of the device.",
  "Base of 5th": "The bony prominence on the outer midfoot at the base of the fifth metatarsal. A common lateral pressure point.",
  "Forefoot": "Anterior region: toe tips through the 1st\u20135th metatarsal heads.",
  "Midfoot": "Arch region: from the metatarsal heads toward the calcaneus.",
  "Rearfoot": "Heel region: the calcaneus and posterior structures.",
  "Functional": "A device that uses a more pronounced arch and contouring to influence how the foot moves.",
  "Diabetic": "A device whose priority is broad, even contact and pressure redistribution when protective sensation is reduced.",
  "distal / proximal": "Directions along the body. Distal = farther from the center (the toes are distal to the ankle); proximal = closer to it (the ankle is proximal to the toes).",
  "plantar surface": "The bottom of the foot \u2014 the sole. The surface every insole is shaped against, and the surface the top cover finishes.",
  "MPJ": "Metatarsophalangeal joint \u2014 where a toe meets its foot bone. The big-toe joint is the 1st MPJ, the most heavily loaded joint in the forefoot.",
  "bilateral": "Affecting both feet. A bilateral prescription builds the same modification into the left and the right device.",
  "flange": "A vertical wall extended up the side of an orthotic \u2014 medial, lateral, or both \u2014 to stop the foot sliding sideways. The defining feature of a UCBL. SoleGen has insole flanges and shell flanges; they are the same switch, different starting heights.",
  "posting": "Adding material to tilt the device: rearfoot posting wedges the heel, forefoot posting tilts the front through the trimline. A printed medial or lateral wedge is a separate slab under the insole; a Kirby Skive is a facet pressed into the heel from the bed side.",
  "heel post": "A platform built onto the underside of the shell under the heel. Four shapes, one per device: Oval (the usual even correction), Flat (height without tilt), U (center of the heel unloaded), and Stabilizer (the widest, most controlling base).",
  "Kirby Skive": "A steep medial heel bias printed into the shell \u2014 aggressive pronation control at first contact, more abrupt than a standard medial rearfoot post. No separate wedge part.",
  "gait plate": "An insole whose front edge is cut back to the met line and carried distally on one side, so the foot rolls off over the other side. In-toeing carries the lateral side; out-toeing carries the medial. Mutually exclusive with the ray cutouts.",
  "thickness boost": "The shell prints thicker than standard across the whole plate. No angle, no directional correction \u2014 just more material, so the device is heavier and more rigid.",
  "arch reinforcement": "Stiffens the medial wall under the arch by raising the wall angle. No extra part \u2014 the studio just switches it on. Different from an arch pad, which is a soft insert.",
  "sensory bumps": "Sixteen half-sunk sphere caps on the plantar surface: five along the toes, two diagonals of three down the midfoot, five on the heel. Same anatomical pattern on every foot; diameter scales with size.",
  "Dannenburg Wedge": "A soft forefoot region printed at its own density (also called a kinetic wedge). The rear boundary is five draggable points. The whole local thickness prints soft. Independent \u2014 replaces nothing.",
  "reverse dancer's pad": "The mirror-side companion to a dancer's pad: cut-out around the 5th met head instead of the 1st, so MT1\u20134 carry load and MT5 floats. Mutually exclusive with met pad and met bar.",
  "PT groove": "A long oval relief along the peroneus / plantar-tendon course in the central arch \u2014 a nodule accommodation, slightly medial. Same cutter family as a custom offload.",
  "tarsal tunnel": "The tunnel behind the medial malleolus. A Tarsal Tunnel Groove scoops the medial heel-cup wall so that nerve and tendon have room; the insole body itself is untouched.",
  "heel fill": "Extra material built up on the heel seat, thickest at center, feathering with no step. The opposite of a heel pad: the pad cuts a soft insert into the thickness; the fill builds the seat up. Shares a front-edge control with the heel pad.",
  "ray cutout": "A bite taken out of a shell's front edge. 1st ray = a round medial bite behind MT1. 5th ray = a straight oblique cut on the lateral front edge. Independent of each other; mutually exclusive with a gait plate.",
  "donut pad": "A circular pad with a hole in the middle, glued around a painful spot (a corn or a sore) so the spot itself touches nothing.",
  "heel spur": "A bony growth on the underside of the heel bone, usually paired with plantar fasciitis. Offloaded with a relief well under the spur point.",
  "accommodation": "A modification added to an orthotic base to address one area of the foot that needs extra relief, support, or cushioning. The base manages the whole foot; accommodations work locally.",
  "Bi-Lam": "Two-layer diabetic construction. \u201cBi\u201d = two. A foam base plus one top cover. Inside Hike, the finished Bi-Lam is called a Sweet.",
  "Tri-Lam": "Three-layer diabetic construction. \u201cTri\u201d = three. A firmer base, a mid-layer, and a top cover. Inside Hike, the finished Tri-Lams are called Double Sweet or Triple Sweet.",
  "Sweet": "Hike\u2019s internal company name for a finished Bi-Lam insole \u2014 a 35-durometer base with its 1/8\u2033 P-Cell top cover (T1) already on. The name describes that whole device.",
  "Double Sweet": "Hike\u2019s internal company name for a finished mid-range Tri-Lam \u2014 a 45-durometer base with a 1/16\u2033 Poron mid-layer and a 1/16\u2033 P-Cell top cover (T2). The name describes that whole device.",
  "Triple Sweet": "Hike\u2019s internal company name for a finished firm Tri-Lam \u2014 a 55-durometer base with a 1/16\u2033 Poron mid-layer and a 1/8\u2033 P-Cell top cover (T3). The name describes that whole device.",
  "BYO": "Build Your Own. Each of the three layers \u2014 base, mid-layer, top cover \u2014 is specified independently on Empire Medical. Used for VA and private custom orders.",
  "EVA": "A durable mid-soft foam used as a diabetic base (35 / 45 / 55 durometer) or as a BYO base / mid-layer.",
  "Puff": "A soft cushioning top cover. The standard comfort layer on a UCBL (1/8\u2033 Puff).",
  "Leather": "A smooth, durable top-cover option. Reduces friction; used on sensitive skin and some BYO orders.",
  "Cork": "A natural structural base. Distributes pressure, manages moisture, and is easy to grind after the fact.",
};

